            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. I observed air leakage around your doors, and at the connections between walls and between the walls and ceilings. I would also recommend checking the connection to the utilidor in the crawlspace. Although I was not able to check this with the IR camera during the test, this is a common location for air leakage. 



I also noted a fair bit of air leakage around the attic hatch. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The main exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, and the walls of your mechanical shed appear to be 4x4 studs with R12 fibreglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to all the walls of your home.



Typically, upgrading exterior wall insulation is done at the same time as a siding replacement.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1175&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;29.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house has 10 inches (R32) of insulation. Some of this is fibreglass batt insulation and some of this is old vermiculite insulation. I have modelled adding enough insulation so that you have R50 in your attic.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your mechanical shed appears to have 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, polyisocyanurate insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam, polyisocyanurate insulation to the cathedral ceiling, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $20&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program if you do this at the same time time as you insulate the main attic of your house.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I recommend that you replace your gas-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your gas-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $285&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current gas fired boiler was originally rated at 83.0% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing gas boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



I also recommend adding an electronic damper on your combusion air pipe, to insure that combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing gas boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $890&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



For this report, I have modelled a 3kW solar photovoltaic system. Depending on your roof, you may be able to install a larger system, or you may need to install a smaller system. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $735&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;

Your windows are already triple paned, hinged windows that appear to have a Low E coating on them. As such I do not recommend replacing any of your windows at this time. However, should you ever need to replace a window for any other reason such as physical damage, look for ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 that are triple paned, double Low E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.  



&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Inuvik&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Natural gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Wood - $472.50 per cord 

Pellets - $11.25 per bag



